If a dedicated Android Canvas View or custom rendering component provides better performance, it can be used inside a Compose container.

The rendering implementation must remain compatible with the native Kotlin application.

Do not turn the entire game into a web page.

## 15.3 Simulation engine

The simulation engine must be independent of the UI.

Create separate modules or packages for:

- TrackGraph.
- TrackNode.
- TrackSegment.
- JunctionSystem.
- SignalSystem.
- TrackOccupancyManager.
- RoutePlanner.
- InterlockingSystem.
- DeadlockDetector.
- TrainController.
- TrainMovement.
- TrainSchedule.
- StationManager.
- PlatformManager.
- RailwaySimulation.
- ScenarioManager.
- ScoreManager.

These represent logical responsibilities, not a requirement to create an excessive number of tiny files.

Use sensible modular boundaries.

The simulation must never depend on Compose rendering state to determine whether a train has occupied a section of railway.

## 15.4 Simulation timing

Use a fixed simulation timestep.

Separate simulation timing from rendering frame rate.

Pausing the game must pause simulation state.

Changing simulation speed must not corrupt train positions.

Use appropriate coroutine or game-loop mechanisms.

Avoid tying railway calculations to arbitrary UI recompositions.

## 15.5 Railway geometry

Store track connectivity independently of screen coordinates.

Use a consistent transformation between logical network coordinates and rendered coordinates.

Support camera pan and zoom.

Ensure switches, rails, trains and station platforms align correctly.

Use reusable rendering primitives and sprite assets.

## 15.6 State management

Represent simulation state using explicit, testable data models.

The UI should observe the relevant state.

Do not make UI components responsible for modifying unrelated railway simulation internals.

Ensure that train selection, route information and operational status always reflect the real simulation state.

## 15.7 Performance

Optimise for ordinary Android devices.

Aim for smooth gameplay near 60 FPS where possible.

Avoid unnecessary object allocation inside frequent rendering and simulation loops.

Use efficient asset management.

Only update visual state when required.

Avoid excessive recomposition.

Do not implement expensive environmental effects that provide little gameplay value.

